Computer Vision on Azure

how machines see, the computer vision task taxonomy, and Azure's vision services.

How a computer "sees" :
As we Humans see by interpreting light through eyes and the brain. Machines “see” by converting images/videos into numerical data (pixels) and using algorithms or AI models to interpret patterns.

Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) do this by sliding small learned filters across the image, building understanding hierarchically:
Early layers → edges and gradients
Middle layers → textures and shapes (ears, wheels)
Late layers → whole objects ("cat", "car")

Computer Vision Task Taxonomy:
Computer vision tasks are grouped by what problem the system solves.
The filters are learned via gradient descent but not programmed. Modern Azure vision uses large multimodal foundation models — one model can caption, tag, and answer questions about images.

Azure services:

Azure AI Vision — image analysis (captions, tags, objects) + OCR via the Read API (printed & handwritten)
Azure AI Face — detection, attributes, verification, identification. Recognition is Limited Access (must apply with use case) — responsible AI principles becoming real product policy
Azure AI Custom Vision — train your own classifier/detector on your own labeled images (~50+/class), no ML expertise needed. Tell: "built-in model doesn't know our parts" → Custom Vision
Azure AI Document Intelligence — form/document → structured fields. Image is a form and you want data → this, not plain OCR

