Key Takeaways:
Machines “see” images as pixel data and use AI models (especially CNNs) to learn patterns like edges, shapes, and objects through hierarchical feature extraction.
Computer vision tasks are grouped by purpose: classification (what), detection (what + where), segmentation (pixel-level), OCR (text extraction), face analysis, captioning, and anomaly detection.
Modern models are trained using deep learning and transfer learning, not manually programmed rules.
Azure AI Vision provides general image analysis, tagging, captioning, object detection, and OCR capabilities.
Azure AI Face handles face detection and identity verification, with some features requiring controlled access due to responsible AI policies.
Azure AI Custom Vision enables training custom image models using small labeled datasets by leveraging transfer learning from pre-trained CNN models.
Azure AI Document Intelligence goes beyond OCR by extracting structured, meaningful data (fields, tables, key-value pairs) from documents like invoices and forms.
Overall, Azure abstracts complex deep learning models into simple API-based services, allowing developers to use computer vision without building models from scratch.
